From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] bash, conhost: bash in a Terminal tab, and full-screen programs there

David 2026-10-04: "if I open up PowerShell directly and type in bash, it
doesn't seem to work". A Terminal tab is a pseudo console: there is no Unix
terminal under it to hand to bash (0781).

bash.exe, interactive in a console with no Unix terminal, gives bash one of
its own: script(1) runs it on a pseudo-terminal, two FIFOs carry the keys
in and the output out (Wine's pipes are the server's; a Unix program cannot
read them), console key events become the keys an xterm sends, the output
is written with the console's escape sequences, the terminal's size follows
the console's (stty -F), and bash's exit status is bash.exe's. Started in
the user's Windows profile -- where a Terminal tab starts -- bash is in the
home folder.

conhost's escape sequences gain what htop and vi need there: the scroll
region (CSI r; line feed, index, insert and delete lines within it) and
repeat (CSI b) -- htop's meters and columns were scattered.

Test: test/bashpty-gate.sh with test/bashpty-probe.c and
test/vtregion-probe.c (mutants SG_MUTANT_BASH_NO_BRIDGE,
SG_MUTANT_VT_NO_REGION); QA VM: nano, htop, vi in a Terminal tab.
